FETCH(1)                       P32W Unix                        FETCH(1)

NAME
       fetch - get a web page or file by HTTP or HTTPS

SYNOPSIS
       fetch [-qv] [-o file | -O] url

DESCRIPTION
       fetch gets url, http://host[:port]/path or https://..., and
       writes what it gets to the standard output, to file with -o,
       or with -O to a file named after the last part of the path
       (index.html for a directory, cut to 28 characters).

       -v prints the server's reply line and headers on the standard
       error. -q prints nothing but errors (otherwise fetch says where
       a redirection went and, with -o or -O, how many bytes it wrote).

       Redirections are followed, five at most. A reply's body is read
       to its Content-Length, in chunks if it is sent chunked, or to
       the end of the connection.

       For https:, the network coprocessor makes the connection with
       TLS and checks the server's certificate against the host name:
       fetch fails if the certificate is not trusted or is for another
       name. fetch itself only speaks HTTP.

EXIT STATUS
       0 for a 2xx reply, 1 for an HTTP error (the reply line is
       printed) or a failure on the way, 2 for a bad command line.

EXAMPLES
       fetch https://example.com/
              a page, to the terminal

       fetch -O http://10.0.2.2:8000/notes.txt
              a file from a web server on the Mac, as notes.txt

SEE ALSO
       telnet(1), socket(2), inet(3)

BUGS
       GET only: no forms, no uploads, no cookies, no compression. No
       proxy. A redirection to another scheme than http: or https: is
       not followed.
